## 1. Basic Thread — Two Ways to Create One

**Way 1: Extending Thread**

```java
// We create a class that "is" a Thread by extending it
public class MyThread extends Thread {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        // This is the code that runs when the thread starts
        // Think of this as the chef's recipe — what they do when they start working
        System.out.println("Thread is running: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(); // Create the thread (hire the chef)
        t1.start();                   // Start it — DON'T call t1.run() directly!
                                      // Calling run() directly would NOT create a new thread
                                      // start() is what actually launches it in parallel
    }
}
```

**Way 2: Implementing Runnable (generally preferred)**

```java
// Runnable is just a "contract" — it says: "this class MUST have a run() method"
public class MyRunnable implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        // Same idea — this is what the thread will do when started
        System.out.println("Runnable thread running: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyRunnable task = new MyRunnable();   // Create the task (the recipe)
        Thread t1 = new Thread(task);         // Wrap it in a Thread (assign it to a chef)
        t1.start();                           // Start the thread
    }
}
```

