## 3. The Bug — Reading Results Too Early (Before Using `join()`)

```java
public class JoinProblemExample {

    static float weatherAvg = 0; // Shared variable — both threads and main can see this
    static float powerAvg = 0;

    static class WeatherThread extends Thread {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(2000); // Simulates time to read file
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
            weatherAvg = 11.0f; // Thread writes the result into the shared variable
        }
    }

    static class PowerThread extends Thread {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
            powerAvg = 456.0f;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WeatherThread wt = new WeatherThread();
        PowerThread pt = new PowerThread();

        wt.start();
        pt.start();

        // BUG: We're reading the results immediately after starting the threads
        // But the threads haven't finished yet — they're still "sleeping" (processing)
        // So weatherAvg and powerAvg are still 0!
        // This is like asking a chef "is the food ready?" 1 second after they started cooking

        if (weatherAvg > powerAvg) {
            System.out.println("Weather avg is higher: " + weatherAvg);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Power avg is higher: " + powerAvg);
        }
        // This will incorrectly print "Power avg is higher: 0.0"
        // Both values are 0 because the threads haven't finished yet
    }
}
```

---

## 4. The Fix — Using `join()` to Wait for Threads

```java
public class JoinSolutionExample {

    static float weatherAvg = 0;
    static float powerAvg = 0;

    static class WeatherThread extends Thread {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(2000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
            weatherAvg = 11.0f;
            System.out.println("WeatherThread finished.");
        }
    }

    static class PowerThread extends Thread {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
            powerAvg = 456.0f;
            System.out.println("PowerThread finished.");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        WeatherThread wt = new WeatherThread();
        PowerThread pt = new PowerThread();

        // Start both threads — they now run at the same time
        wt.start();
        pt.start();

        // join() makes the main program PAUSE here and wait for each thread to finish
        // It's like saying: "Don't move on until this chef is done cooking"
        wt.join(); // Wait for the weather thread to finish
        pt.join(); // Wait for the power thread to finish

        // Only NOW do we read the results — we're guaranteed both threads are done
        System.out.println("Both threads done. Comparing results...");

        if (weatherAvg > powerAvg) {
            System.out.println("Weather avg is higher: " + weatherAvg);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Power avg is higher: " + powerAvg);
        }
        // This correctly prints "Power avg is higher: 456.0"
    }
}

/* Expected output:
   WeatherThread finished.
   PowerThread finished.
   Both threads done. Comparing results...
   Power avg is higher: 456.0
*/
```

