## 5. Task 4 from Your Lecture — Factorial with Two Threads

The factorial of a number is calculated by multiplying all numbers from 1 up to that number. For example, factorial(5) = 1 × 2 × 3 × 4 × 5 = 120. This splits that work across two threads.

```java
public class FactorialThreads {

    // Thread 1 handles the first half of the multiplication
    // e.g. for factorial(6): Thread 1 does 1 × 2 × 3
    static class FirstHalfThread extends Thread {
        int start, end;
        long result = 1; // Stores this thread's partial result

        FirstHalfThread(int start, int end) {
            this.start = start; // Where to start multiplying
            this.end = end;     // Where to stop
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
                result *= i; // Multiply result by each number in the range
            }
            System.out.println("FirstHalfThread: partial result = " + result);
        }
    }

    // Thread 2 handles the second half
    // e.g. for factorial(6): Thread 2 does 4 × 5 × 6
    static class SecondHalfThread extends Thread {
        int start, end;
        long result = 1;

        SecondHalfThread(int start, int end) {
            this.start = start;
            this.end = end;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
                result *= i;
            }
            System.out.println("SecondHalfThread: partial result = " + result);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        int n = 6; // We want to calculate factorial(6) = 720
        int midpoint = n / 2; // Split the work roughly in half — midpoint = 3

        // Thread 1 multiplies numbers 1 to 3 (gives 6)
        FirstHalfThread t1 = new FirstHalfThread(1, midpoint);

        // Thread 2 multiplies numbers 4 to 6 (gives 120)
        SecondHalfThread t2 = new SecondHalfThread(midpoint + 1, n);

        // Start both threads — they work in parallel
        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        // Wait for both threads to finish before we combine results
        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        // Combine: multiply the two partial results together
        // 6 × 120 = 720, which is factorial(6) — correct!
        long finalResult = t1.result * t2.result;
        System.out.println("Factorial(" + n + ") = " + finalResult);
    }
}

/* Expected output:
   FirstHalfThread: partial result = 6
   SecondHalfThread: partial result = 120
   Factorial(6) = 720
*/
```

---

### Quick Reference

|What you write|What it does|
|---|---|
|`extends Thread`|Makes your class a thread|
|`implements Runnable`|Another way — puts task code in a class|
|`run()`|Where the thread's code lives|
|`start()`|Actually launches the thread in parallel|
|`join()`|Makes the caller wait for that thread to finish|
|`Thread.sleep(ms)`|Pauses the current thread for a number of milliseconds|

The most important thing to remember: **always call `start()` not `run()`**, and **always use `join()` before you read results from a thread**.


